<description>
Fill every row from the nearest marked horizontal bar, turning bar rows and color-boundary ties into separator rows.

Input structure
- A dominant background fills the grid except for full-width horizontal bars and one vertical marker column.
- Outside the marker column, every row is monochrome: either background or a source bar color.
- Background rows share one marker color in the marker column; source bar rows share the other.

Rule
- Each non-source row takes the color of the nearest source bar, with that row's marker-column pixel set to the source-marker color.
- If a row is exactly midway between nearest bars of different colors, make the whole row the source-marker color.
- Each original source bar row becomes a source-marker row with the marker-column pixel changed to the background-row marker color.

Key insight
- The marked bars are horizontal seeds for a one-dimensional nearest-seed partition over rows; equal-distance rows are separators only when the competing seed colors differ.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="45a5af55" lines="5-18">
<description>
Build nested square frames from a stack of horizontal color stripes.

Input structure:
- Full-width horizontal stripes of distinct non-bg colors, stacked vertically.
- Each stripe has uniform color and variable height (1-4 rows).

Rule:
- Read stripes bottom to top.
- Bottom stripe becomes the center solid square with side length = stripe height.
- Each subsequent stripe wraps the current shape with a square frame of thickness = stripe height, in that stripe's color.

Key insight: Stripe height doubles as both frame thickness and (for the innermost) center-square side length; output dimensions are fully determined by the stripe heights.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="8b7bacbf" lines="42-56">
<description>
Fill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.
- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.

Rule:
- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.
- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.
- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.

Key insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="f560132c" lines="259-273">
<description>
Assemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the "pieces"), scattered across the grid.
- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.

Rule:
- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.
- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.
- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.

Key insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="train" puzzle_id="57edb29d" lines="88-103">
<description>
Extract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.

Input structure:
- The boundary color is the background/separator.
- Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.
- Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.

Rule:
- Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.
- Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.
- Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.

Key insight:
- The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="eval" puzzle_id="9385bd28" lines="71-87">
<description>
Fill rectangles between diagonal marker pairs using a color legend.

Input structure:
- bg grid with a vertical legend at the bottom-left: each row is a (marker_color, fill_color) pair.
- Scattered L-shaped corner markers of each marker color define opposite corners of axis-aligned rectangles.
- Some markers are solid 2×2 blocks instead of corner pairs.

Rule:
- Read legend bottom-to-top; for each entry find all pixels of that marker color.
- Determine which two bbox corners carry L-shaped anchors — the *other* diagonal defines the fill region.

